{
  "blocks": [
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "text": "Binary search is usually taught as a way to find a value in a sorted array. That is the smallest version of it."
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "text": "The bigger idea: binary search works on **any range where a yes/no question flips exactly once**. Often that range is not the array at all — it is the space of possible answers."
    },
    {
      "type": "heading",
      "text": "The reframing"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "text": "Questions like \"what is the minimum capacity that finishes the job in time?\" look like optimisation problems. But turn them around:"
    },
    {
      "type": "code",
      "language": "text",
      "code": "instead of:  what is the smallest capacity that works?\nask:         does capacity X work?   (yes / no)"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "text": "The second question is usually easy — simulate it and see. And if the answers are **monotonic** — once yes, always yes for anything larger — then binary search finds the boundary."
    },
