    {
      "type": "heading",
      "text": "Why monotonicity is required"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "text": "Binary search discards half the range on every step. That is only sound if the answer at the midpoint tells you which half to keep."
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "text": "If capacity 10 works, any larger capacity must also work — so everything above 10 is redundant and can be dropped. That inference is exactly what monotonicity guarantees. Without it, a midpoint says nothing about its neighbours and the halving is invalid."
    },
    {
      "type": "heading",
      "text": "The skeleton"
    },
    {
      "type": "code",
      "language": "javascript",
      "code": "let low = smallestPossibleAnswer;\nlet high = largestPossibleAnswer;\n\nwhile (low < high) {\n  const mid = Math.floor((low + high) / 2);\n\n  if (canDo(mid)) {\n    high = mid;        // mid works — it might be the answer, so keep it\n  } else {\n    low = mid + 1;     // mid fails — the answer is strictly larger\n  }\n}\nreturn low;"
    },
    {
      "type": "heading",
      "text": "The detail that trips everyone"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "text": "Note the asymmetry: `high = mid` but `low = mid + 1`."
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "text": "When `mid` works it is still a candidate, so it must stay in the range. When `mid` fails it is definitively ruled out, so we skip past it. Writing `high = mid - 1` discards a valid answer; writing `low = mid` makes the loop spin forever when `low` and `high` are adjacent."
    },
    {
      "type": "heading",
      "text": "Cost"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "text": "**O(log R x C)**, where R is the size of the answer range and C is the cost of one check. The log factor is tiny — a range of a billion needs only thirty checks."
    }
  ],
